#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125311} :activity/id #uuid "2bfe5aae-e1d5-50d4-96df-8e775e21203d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/id #uuid "c8e11bf4-259c-5464-94a3-b5c5d6409c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125741 :tutorial/content "The partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x,y) = \\ln(xy).$ We wish to determine the set of values $(x,y)$ on which the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists.\n\nFirst, we need to find the domain of $f$ since we cannot differentiate $f$ at any point that lies outside of its domain. The natural logarithm is defined only for positive inputs, so the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy > 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ (also denoted $f_x$) is not defined at $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_x})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy \\gt 0 \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5862db65-6f2d-5256-968d-cb971dd6b2f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2532 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e4bac5fb-b77d-59b1-8380-2ff0cf754d5c" :step/math-academy-id 11425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124269 :question/id #uuid "a663f68e-1fb9-5653-a128-1b55c2a58769" :question/math-academy-id "e-3449"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382200}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"} :step/id #uuid "d81b9ea9-a347-5ebb-91d1-4bc5c4b566e0" :step/math-academy-id 11426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124270 :question/id #uuid "7297f213-2db2-5da2-9bba-cac7e31f6b64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"} :step/id #uuid "f4cc9d43-0b3e-5cd2-8e04-c1f0f43e9e2a" :step/math-academy-id 11429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125742 :tutorial/content "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n$$f(x,y)= {\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\, & (x,y)\\ne (0,0) \\\\ 0, & (x,y)=(0,0)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$ $$f(0,0)=0$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\ & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)} f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\ & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca47070b-a1d3-53ad-8f57-2dbd4e770dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2533 :tutorial/title "Partial Differentiability Does Not Imply Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "f23e88d8-c204-5795-b172-b05000a202c3" :step/math-academy-id 11432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124366 :question/id #uuid "97192aac-6c1a-5ead-962b-0bf7ab573661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382207}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"} :step/id #uuid "c86dfe84-cedf-55cf-bf44-efb12a49ac66" :step/math-academy-id 22675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125313}}] :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124725}]}
